WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.297
<v><b>Speaker:</b></v> ‘One day I’d like to do a big adventure.’

00:00:03.993 --> 00:00:10.432
I hear this all the time, at parties, at events I speak at, by email from strangers.

00:00:11.088 --> 00:00:15.305
‘But I don’t have the time / money / fitness / shiny kit.’

00:00:16.195 --> 00:00:20.128
The excuses vary occasionally, but the essence remains the same:

00:00:20.745 --> 00:00:26.206
‘One day I want adventure in my life, but, unfortunately, it can’t be right now’.

00:00:27.528 --> 00:00:31.983
Waiting for all your stars to align is a guaranteed way to ensure

00:00:32.008 --> 00:00:34.845
that the adventure you crave will never happen.

00:00:35.588 --> 00:00:39.308
Waiting until you somehow, suddenly and simultaneously,

00:00:39.666 --> 00:00:43.864
have both loads of money and plenty of time is daft

00:00:44.164 --> 00:00:49.029
(if you’ll excuse me being a bit direct before we have got to know one another properly).

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.197
One day! What rubbish!

00:00:52.907 --> 00:00:59.535
If this applies to you, listen! You do not need a winning lottery ticket to have an adventure.

00:01:00.081 --> 00:01:04.402
What you need is a polite kick up the backside! A push.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.003
I want this book to give you the tiny bit of momentum needed to get started.

00:01:10.877 --> 00:01:13.850
This is a book for people who want adventure in their life,

00:01:14.251 --> 00:01:16.667
but find that real life has got in the way.

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:25.791
‘One day’ is just an excuse. It’s lazy, self-deluding and – worst of all – completely unnecessary.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.688
So if you are procrastinating and dithering about committing to adventure,

00:01:31.238 --> 00:01:33.662
why don’t you begin with a one-day adventure,

00:01:34.213 --> 00:01:38.287
something so tiny that it can barely be called an adventure?

00:01:38.938 --> 00:01:46.489
Let’s call it a ‘microadventure’. Now go and do it as soon as you can, not ‘one day’...

00:01:46.897 --> 00:01:53.604
Tomorrow would be a good time to start. Or this weekend at the very latest. Start small.

00:01:53.938 --> 00:01:55.193
But do start.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:00.185
The concept is simple: Pick up your wallet. You’re ready.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:04.550
Open the door, step out…and begin.

00:02:05.503 --> 00:02:09.702
(This sentence, by the way, is the hardest thing you will read in this entire book.)

00:02:10.689 --> 00:02:15.562
Then jump on a train. I’d suggest doing this microadventure by bike,

00:02:15.890 --> 00:02:19.198
because you can explore so much more new ground that way,

00:02:19.655 --> 00:02:24.268
but going by foot is a simple alternative. Hell, take a canoe if you like.

00:02:25.316 --> 00:02:31.274
Anyway, choose a station, almost at random, that’s about 30 miles away from your home.

00:02:32.023 --> 00:02:33.367
Buy a one-way ticket.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:39.420
I say 30 miles because it’s a distance long enough to be challenging on a bike,

00:02:39.818 --> 00:02:43.500
but achievable in one day, even if you are pretty unfit.

00:02:44.266 --> 00:02:48.341
If you’re on foot or if you can’t manage 30 miles, then do a shorter trip.

00:02:48.825 --> 00:02:54.743
But don’t just duck out and do no trip! This approach applies throughout this book:

00:02:55.319 --> 00:02:58.994
if something I suggest is too difficult, do an easier version.

00:02:59.468 --> 00:03:05.233
If something is too easy, make it harder. Mould it and adapt it to your own situation.

00:03:05.787 --> 00:03:07.754
Just make sure you do something.

00:03:09.088 --> 00:03:12.973
When you arrive at your destination, hop on your bike and cycle home.

00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:17.373
Travelling 30 miles away from your home should guarantee

00:03:17.398 --> 00:03:20.126
that you’ll begin the adventure in lovely countryside.

00:03:21.079 --> 00:03:24.003
I suggest you navigate homewards using a compass,

00:03:24.257 --> 00:03:27.499
or the Crowsflight app on your smartphone, rather than a map.

00:03:28.398 --> 00:03:31.967
A compass (or the app) won’t tell you what route to take,

00:03:32.291 --> 00:03:34.666
it will only point you in the right direction.

00:03:35.447 --> 00:03:38.945
The twists and turns and decisions that crop up along the way are

00:03:39.293 --> 00:03:42.394
therefore down to your instinct and serendipity.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.107
It becomes more of a journey into the unknown

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:51.431
than merely following the clearly prescribed route back home that a map would give you.

00:03:52.488 --> 00:03:57.505
If that feels a bit daunting, by all means use a map or even a satnav.

00:03:58.293 --> 00:04:04.032
Don’t let any of the ideas in this book put you off! Just modify them to what appeals to you.

00:04:04.824 --> 00:04:07.615
I once spent four years cycling back home.

00:04:08.205 --> 00:04:13.512
Within the single day ahead of you will lie most of the challenges, hurdles and rewards,

00:04:13.751 --> 00:04:19.039
of a multi-year ride – although maybe fewer bears. But I mean this seriously.

00:04:19.762 --> 00:04:21.703
You will have to navigate your way,

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:28.233
you’ll become wildly hungry and relish the joy of stopping at a café and eating cake, guilt free.

00:04:28.971 --> 00:04:31.289
You will see things you have never seen before.

00:04:32.173 --> 00:04:37.427
If you travel slowly and with a smile on your face, then you will meet different people,

00:04:37.516 --> 00:04:42.315
have interesting conversations and learn something new about the world and about yourself.

00:04:43.402 --> 00:04:48.133
When you eventually make it back to your front door, you’ll be tired, aching, and hungry.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:53.371
But you will have had an unusual and rewarding day and – I sincerely hope

00:04:53.713 --> 00:04:58.400
– you will have realized that you are capable of more and are thus eager for more.

00:04:58.904 --> 00:05:01.195
You have opened the door to adventure.